-- Rules that start themselves again where L4 could not check that this comes
-- to an end, beside rules of the same shapes that it could check, and the
-- @ends-because marks that answer the warning. Recursion-limit ruling
-- L3 (2026-11-20) asks for a positive and a negative fixture for
-- each shape a warning fires on; ruling L4 says where a mark counts. Both are
-- described in doc/reference/regulative/rules-that-start-themselves-again.md,
-- and the golden pins the text of each message.

DECLARE Person IS ONE OF Alex, Sam
DECLARE Action IS ONE OF `pay rent`, `pay a fee`

-- Warns: the time allowed is `days`, not a number written out.
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`reminder` days MEANS
    PARTY Alex
    MUST `pay rent`
    WITHIN days
    LEST `reminder` (days DIVIDED BY 2)

-- No warning: WITHIN 7 is a number written out, so each copy waits.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`weekly reminder` MEANS
    PARTY Alex
    MUST `pay rent`
    WITHIN 7
    LEST `weekly reminder`

-- No warning: the mark above it answers the warning, and leaves a note.
@ends-because tries drops by one at every reminder, and at 0 there are no more reminders
GIVEN tries IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`final reminders` tries MEANS
    IF tries AT MOST 0
    THEN BREACH
    ELSE PARTY Alex
         MUST `pay rent`
         WITHIN tries TIMES 7
         LEST `final reminders` (tries MINUS 1)

-- Warns, on `first notice`, the first of the two in the file: the mark above
-- `second notice` does not count, and the warning and its note say so.
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`first notice` days MEANS
    PARTY Alex
    MUST `pay rent`
    WITHIN days
    LEST `second notice` days

@ends-because Sam pays before the second notice runs out
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`second notice` days MEANS
    PARTY Sam
    MUST `pay rent`
    WITHIN days
    LEST `first notice` days

-- No warning: the mark above `call one` answers it; the one above `call two`
-- does not count, and its note says that the first one answers the warning.
@ends-because Alex pays by the third call
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`call one` days MEANS
    PARTY Alex
    MUST `pay rent`
    WITHIN days
    LEST `call two` days

@ends-because Alex pays by the third call
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`call two` days MEANS
    PARTY Alex
    MUST `pay rent`
    WITHIN days
    LEST `call one` days

-- Warns: besides its LEST, the rule uses itself directly, and `n EQUALS 0`
-- is not a stop that `n MINUS 1` is sure to reach (marks.l4 has the same rule
-- with `n AT MOST 0`, which L4 can check).
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
`instalments` n MEANS
    IF n EQUALS 0
    THEN FULFILLED
    ELSE (PARTY Alex MUST `pay rent` WITHIN 30 LEST `instalments` (n MINUS 1))
         RAND `instalments` (n MINUS 1)
